From ae3897657d0513f3667fdad20082a4d35ce99680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 21:15:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common: size -fit's auto-context probe from measured free
 memory, not just a caught exception

70f0d0168 made -fit catch the KV-cache-tensor-too-large exception
(ggml_backend_dev_supports_op rejecting an oversized per-op tensor,
e.g. Vulkan's maxStorageBufferRange) and back off geometrically. That
covers a discrete GPU with a low per-op ceiling but no other memory
surprises. On a UMA device the per-op check is not the problem: each
layer's K/V tensor can pass it individually while the KV cache still
adds up to more real memory than the device can back once actually
allocated and zero-initialized.

Reproduced locally on this workstation's Intel UHD 770 iGPU (Vulkan,
UMA -- same class of device as the gfx90c/680M this was found on,
per QA round 8 section R1b): K2-Horizon-7B with no -c, so llama-server
uses its huge native n_ctx_train (524288) times n_parallel=auto. The
existing fix's per-op check does reject the first probe (2097152) and
backs off once to 1048576, exactly as designed -- and that is where it
stops, because backing off further needs a second failure to catch,
and none was thrown. At that size the real KV-cache buffer is nominally
allocated fine, but zero-initializing it (llama-kv-cache.cpp's
ggml_backend_buffer_clear right after allocation) touches pages the
device can't really back. On this UMA backend a host-visible buffer's
clear is a plain CPU memset (ggml_vk_buffer_memset), so the failure
surfaces as the kernel's own page-fault path spinning forever
(dmesg floods with "VM_FAULT_OOM leaked out to the #PF handler",
never terminating -- worse than a crash, no graceful anything). QA's
report of the same bug on gfx90c hit the GPU-fillBuffer variant of the
same clear operation instead, surfacing as a real vk::DeviceLostError
during command submission. Same root cause, two different failure
shapes depending on whether the backend's buffer-clear path happens to
be host-visible-and-UMA or not; neither is something a per-op support
check could ever catch, because the tensor itself was never the
problem.

Fix (option a from the brief): before the auto-context probe ever
tries the full n_ctx_max, take one cheap real measurement at
n_ctx_min_total (always small enough to be safe) to get a real
bytes-per-ctx figure, then clamp the probe so projected KV-cache use
stays inside each device's free memory minus its existing --fit
margin -- the same free-memory accounting the rest of this function
already does further down for its own post-probe sizing, just applied
before building anything instead of after a failure. For an
integrated/UMA device, also cap its free-memory figure by the host's
own reported free bytes: a UMA device's GTT pool is carved from host
RAM, and (per this fleet's own prior finding on AMD APUs) its "free"
figure is not guaranteed to reflect what every other process on the
host is actually holding. The existing per-op-exception backoff loop
is kept, unchanged, as defense in depth for whatever the linear
estimate misses -- it is a linear extrapolation off one small
measurement, not exact.

Options b and c from the brief were considered and not used as the
fix. (b): the probe path does not actually need to stop submitting
GPU work, because it already does not -- llama_model_params.no_alloc
routes llama-kv-cache.cpp to a dummy 0-byte buffer during a measurement
pass, skipping both the real allocation and the clear. The clear that
actually crashes runs during the real (post-fit) load, which every
device has to do regardless of --fit; there is nothing to remove
there. (c): making the Vulkan backend turn a submit failure into a
catchable error only helps the gfx90c shape. It does nothing for the
Intel iGPU shape reproduced here, which is a kernel-level page-fault
stall inside a plain CPU memset, not a C++ exception anything in
ggml-vulkan.cpp is on the stack for. ggml-vulkan.cpp already logs and
rethrows vk::DeviceLostError from its own submit paths (see
ggml_vk_wait_for_fence, vk_queue_handle_*::submit), which is sufficient
existing defense in depth for that one shape.

Out of scope: the n_ctx_auto && n_seq_max == 1 case (--parallel 1, no
multiplier) runs its one and only probe with zero exception protection
at all -- not new, not touched here, and not what the reported bug or
its verification exercise. Restructuring that would need querying a
model's n_ctx_train before ever building a context at all, a bigger
change than this fix's scope.

Verification (workstation only, per the session's hard rule against
touching cluster nodes after an earlier node-crash incident this
session -- every run below wrapped in
`systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=10G -p MemorySwapMax=0`, one process
at a time, `free -g` checked before each):
- Before this commit, on the Intel UHD 770 iGPU: K2-Horizon-7B with no
  -c backs off once (2097152 -> 1048576) exactly as 70f0d0168 intends,
  then hangs forever with dmesg flooding "VM_FAULT_OOM leaked out to
  the #PF handler" (310+ lines/5s) instead of crashing or recovering.
  Killed by PID after capturing the log.
- After this commit, same case, same cap: logs
  "clamping the auto-context probe from 2097152 to 13863", loads
  cleanly, and serves a real /v1/chat/completions request
  (n_ctx_slot=13312 across 4 slots). The 10G cap needed a matching
  -fitt/--fit-target margin (13000 MiB) to reflect this test's actual
  memory ceiling, because the clamp trusts the device's own reported
  free memory (~19-20 GiB, unaffected by this process's own cgroup) --
  the same way a real deployment sizes -fitt from its container's
  actual memory limit.
- Explicit -c 8192 (same model, same cap): identical before and after
  this commit -- no clamp log line (the new code is gated on
  n_ctx_auto, which -c disables), n_ctx_slot=8192 exactly as requested,
  serves a real completion.
- Could not verify the original 70f0d0168 NVIDIA RTX 3080 Ti case in
  this session: nvidia-smi and Vulkan both report no NVIDIA device
  available (driver not loaded in this sandboxed session). The fix
  does not touch src/llama-kv-cache.cpp or the per-op exception path
  70f0d0168 added, so that regression test is structurally unaffected
  by this change; this is not a substitute for actually running it.
- Could not re-verify on gabesrv01's real gfx90c: an earlier attempt
  this session to reproduce this same bug there ran the node out of
  memory (unbounded -c probe, no cap) and took gabesrv01 NotReady,
  requiring a manual reboot. Per the coordinator's instruction after
  that incident, no further cluster-node testing was done for this
  fix; all verification above is workstation-only.
- ctest -L main: 60/60 (was 59; +1 for the new test below).
- ctest -R split-balance: 1/1.
- Added tests/test-fit-clamp.cpp, a new test file (see AGENTS.md's
  bar for that -- explicitly requested for this fix, and this fork's
  own maintainer is the one asking): direct unit tests of the new
  common_fit_clamp_ctx_to_free_memory() pure function extracted from
  the clamp logic (no free memory, tight budget, zero bytes_per_ctx,
  floor behavior, floor-division rounding).

